Implements the doc-prescribed correction (b): graph_explore_solve_v2 now
takes an optional heuristic=goal_distance (+heuristic_weight, +stats). When
given, the frontier is ordered A*-style by depth + weight*heuristic(frame)
(heap) instead of FIFO. The depth (g) term is precisely why greedy v3
(pure-h) fails on cn04 — A* keeps v2's COMPLETENESS (no local-min trap)
while letting the heuristic prioritize. When heuristic is None the path is
byte-for-byte the original pure-BFS: zero regression (20 graph-explore
tests pass; the proven 8/11 solves are on the None path). stats records
expansions/states so an A/B can measure the EFFICIENCY win (fewer states to
the same win) that action-count misses on shortest-path games.

arc_gap_fill.py now A/Bs the captured heuristic through this v2 path (not
the greedy v3 that the prior note showed cannot solve cn04 regardless), and
credits 'helped' on fewer-actions OR (equal-actions AND fewer-expansions).

Empirical finding (naive Hamming (grid!=WIN).sum() vs BFS, all solve+repro):
 cd82 A* 955exp vs BFS 525 (worse); sp80 A* 1085/6-act vs BFS 301/4-act
 (worse + longer path); su15 A* 1406 vs BFS 1746 (helped, -340 exp).
=> the A* ENGINE is correct; heuristic QUALITY is the real gap. Hamming is
too crude (inadmissible -> suboptimal on sp80). This sharpens the LLM-gap-
filler value: the LLM must write a STRUCTURE-aware heuristic (now measurable
via this A/B + reproduction gate); the gate correctly refuses to bank a
non-helping heuristic so autolearning compounds only verified wins.
cn04 does NOT solve from plain v2-L0 @8000 (mask or not) — its registry
solve is a captured trajectory; wrong A/B target for plain v2.
